---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { attorneys } from '../data/attorneys';
import { helpStates, helpChecked } from '../data/freeHelp';
import { stateLawChecked } from '../data/cities';

const helpTotal = helpStates.reduce((n, s) => n + s.count, 0);
---

<PageLayout
  title="About This Directory | How We List Attorneys and Check State Law"
  description="How ExpungementAttorneys.org chooses the attorneys it lists, where its state law summaries and free legal help listings come from, and how to send a correction."
  canonicalURL="/about"
>
  <div class="container py-8">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "About" }]} />
  </div>

  <section class="py-12 bg-white">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-6">About This Directory</h1>

        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p>
            ExpungementAttorneys.org is an independent directory for people who want to clear a criminal record. It lists attorneys who handle expungement and record sealing, free and low-cost legal help in every state, and a plain-English summary of each state's rules. It is not a law firm and does not give legal advice.
          </p>

          <h2>How attorneys get listed</h2>
          <p>
            {`We list ${attorneys.length} law firms.`} A firm is listed only when there is evidence that it does record-clearing work: its own website describes expungement, record sealing or a similar service, or clients describe that work in public reviews. Firms that showed neither were removed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each profile is written from what the firm publishes on its own website: the services it names, its other practice areas, its lawyers, and practical details such as a free consultation or payment plans. We do not rank firms, and the order on a page is not a recommendation. Ratings shown are the firm's rating on Google, labelled as such.
          </p>
          <p>
            Listing is free. Attorneys do not pay to appear here, and no firm has paid for a better position.
          </p>

          <h2>Where the state law summaries come from</h2>
          <p>
            {`Every state summary was checked in ${stateLawChecked}`} against the Collateral Consequences Resource Center's state profiles and at least one official source, such as the state courts, the legislature or a state agency. Each state page lists the sources used. Where a point could not be confirmed, it was left out.
          </p>
          <p>
            Record-clearing law changes often, and eligibility depends on the details of a case. Treat the summaries as a starting point and confirm with a legal aid office, the court clerk or an attorney in that state.
          </p>

          <h2>Where the free legal help listings come from</h2>
          <p>
            {`The ${helpTotal} free and low-cost programmes`} are legal aid organisations, law school clinics, public defender and court programmes, and nonprofits. Each entry was taken from the organisation's own page or an official listing, and every link was checked to load{helpChecked && ` in ${helpChecked}`}. Programmes change their intake rules and clinic dates, so check with the organisation directly.
          </p>

          <h2>Corrections</h2>
          <p>
            If something here is wrong or out of date, or if you represent a firm or programme and want a listing changed or removed, <a href="/contact">tell us</a> and we will fix it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
